WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.660
SIERRA: Hey Everyone. Usually our show begins by&nbsp;
introducing a topic or a story that you don’t hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:15.900
about every day. Well, that’s clearly not the case&nbsp;
when it comes to Putin’s invasion of Ukraine. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:22.680
entire world is watching, minute-by-minute. We see&nbsp;
footage of reckless destruction, courageous acts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:28.380
by Ukrainian citizens and Russian protestors.&nbsp;
We hear the stories of refugees as they flee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.060
Ukrainian cities. SIERRA: We’re all thinking&nbsp;
about it. And judging from the conversations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:40.080
I’ve been having with family and friends, people&nbsp;
have some basic questions. Like do sanctions work?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:46.800
Why didn’t we see this coming? And, perhaps&nbsp;
most of all, is this going to turn into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:53.040
world war? SIERRA: I had the same questions. So&nbsp;
I went to my boss, CFR President Richard Haass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:58.500
and I asked him to join me for a chat in our New&nbsp;
York studio. SIERRA: You may remember Dr. Haass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:04.620
from previous episodes, including his analysis of&nbsp;
the U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan. He’s a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:10.440
person to talk to at a time like this. He’s served&nbsp;
under four presidents, and has had four decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:15.600
of experience with conflict and diplomacy on the&nbsp;
world stage. SIERRA: The conversation you’re about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.480
to hear is largely unedited. We hope it answers&nbsp;
some of your questions and gives you a more solid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:27.840
foundation for understanding what’s going on.&nbsp;
SIERRA: I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:40.879
Matters. Today, Ukraine, with Richard Haass. 
SIERRA: Okay. Richard Haass, hello.

00:01:40.879 --> 00:01:42.240
HAASS:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.640
Good to be back.
SIERRA: Welcome back to Why It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.640
Matters. It feels like I only see you when there's&nbsp;
a crisis. We kinda need to change that up.
&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:56.820
HAASS: I do feel like I'm the bad news bear. My&nbsp;
kids for a long time called me daddy downer. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.160
to be serious for a second, look, there's a lot&nbsp;
about history, I was just thinking about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.360
that's good. Think about our lives, the&nbsp;
average person lives decades longer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.500
We have the technology, the vaccines they&nbsp;
came out to deal with something like COVID.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:17.220
Even though we're going to be discussing an attack&nbsp;
on freedom, a lot more people around the world are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:23.340
living with a degree of democracy and freedom than&nbsp;
in almost any other time of history. We're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.180
to be talking perhaps about economic sanctions,&nbsp;
but there's more wealth in the world in more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.980
hands than at any time in history. So G*d knows&nbsp;
there's a long list of problems and we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:38.340
to be talking about the biggest immediate one, but&nbsp;
it's not all bad. But there are some things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:44.940
are bad or really bad. And there are some things&nbsp;
that will not get better by themselves. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:49.740
nothing about history that leads me to think&nbsp;
that good things are inevitable. Good things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.138
tend to happen when well intentioned people,&nbsp;
who are willing and able to act get together-
&nbsp;

00:02:54.138 --> 00:02:56.400
SIERRA: Right, it takes work.
HAASS:... and act and push back against those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:02.401
who are, shall we say, not well intentioned.
SIERRA: Well, so to sort of jump in that way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.401 --> 00:03:07.380
a few days ago, I was heading back into the&nbsp;
city with some of my friends, we kind of looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:14.160
over and all these buildings in New York&nbsp;
were blue and yellow in support of Ukraine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:20.220
And you know it felt very good. It felt&nbsp;
warm. It felt like we were all together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:25.320
supporting these people who are being bullied,&nbsp;
who are losing their homes, losing their lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:35.340
War is horrible, and what I want to understand&nbsp;
today is why is this conflict different? Why does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.560
this seem more urgent to the world? Because let's&nbsp;
be fair, we also see terrible images from wars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:47.460
in Yemen and Syria, so what's with this one?
HAASS: Well, as your question suggests, in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:53.280
ways it's not so different. This is not the first&nbsp;
time we've seen reckless wanton destruction. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:58.140
not the first time we've seen unwarranted wars,&nbsp;
what I call wars of choice. It's not the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:04.380
time we've seen the use of cluster munitions or&nbsp;
other types of bombs that truly constitute war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:08.880
crimes. Indeed, it's not the first time Russia's&nbsp;
done it recently. It does it regularly in Syria.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:15.180
So what is different? One, is that it is happening&nbsp;
in Europe. Europe was the venue of so much history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:21.540
in the 20th century, two World Wars. And then you&nbsp;
had in the aftermath of World War II, the rise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:26.520
what we now call the European Union, began through&nbsp;
the European Coal and Steel Community. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:32.880
was a feeling that by having resolved the question&nbsp;
of the French, German, British relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:39.660
that Europe was going to be safe. And then we had&nbsp;
the Cold War, four decades of U.S. and Soviet led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:46.620
standoff in Europe, and we got through that and&nbsp;
it remained cold. And it ended peacefully on terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:51.480
that were extraordinarily favorable to us. So I&nbsp;
think a lot of people increasingly said, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:56.700
check, we can take Europe off our list." Yes,&nbsp;
we've got to worry about terrorism, 9/11. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:01.200
we've got to worry about the Middle East indeed,&nbsp;
a big part of history of the last three decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:06.480
has taken place in the greater Middle East,&nbsp;
from Afghanistan, to Iraq, to Syria, to Yemen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:12.120
to Libya. But not so much in the terms of conflict&nbsp;
in either Europe. Yes, there was the Balkans and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:17.700
so forth, but not so much in Europe and obviously&nbsp;
not so much in Asia. Not so much in other parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:21.360
of the world. So one, we weren't expecting&nbsp;
it. We thought Europe had reached a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:27.240
place in its historical trajectory or arc. And&nbsp;
then secondly, there's something about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:32.520
which is so stark, because Ukraine is a modern&nbsp;
country. I've spent a lot of time there. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:40.080
a modern democracy. And now what's adding to it&nbsp;
is the degree of brutality. There is a starkness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:46.440
to this. It's not just evil versus good, Russia&nbsp;
versus Ukraine. But there's a medieval quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:52.860
The siege. First, the surrounding of Ukraine with&nbsp;
nearly 200,000 forces. Now the attacks on cities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:58.200
on no clear discrimination between military&nbsp;
and civilian targets. This is positively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:03.240
medieval. Plus one more thing, we're seeing&nbsp;
and reading and hearing about it because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.180
of media and above all social media.
SIERRA: Right. It's all over TikTok.
&nbsp;

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:10.920
HAASS: 100%. So one of the things you've learned&nbsp;
over the last few years is that people don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:16.140
notice certain awful things in the world if&nbsp;
the cameras can't get in. And there were places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:20.400
and times in the Middle East or in Africa&nbsp;
where you just didn't have the access. Well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.820
here we've got all the access in the world.
SIERRA: Right there were already influencers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:29.040
they're just changing to now covering this war.
HAASS: Exactly. So I think for a lot of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:33.780
this has just broken through, and maybe I've&nbsp;
left out some reasons, but it's broken through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.640
in ways that a lot of foreign&nbsp;
policy and international stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.780
just doesn't break through.
SIERRA: Yeah. But why now?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:46.800
Why is Putin doing this now? Why not&nbsp;
two years ago or two years from now?
&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:51.240
HAASS: It's hard to answer with certainty.&nbsp;
Anytime you've got a political system where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:57.060
decision making is concentrated within one&nbsp;
individual, whether it's North Korea or Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:02.820
it's pretty hard to assess much less predict.&nbsp;
Indeed, most people didn't predict we would ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:09.840
be where we are. So why now? So We're here into&nbsp;
assessments and conjectures. One thing I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:15.000
think is in no particular order, opportunity,&nbsp;
he saw the divisions within the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:20.580
We've had several administrations in a row, three&nbsp;
administrations in a row, Obama, Trump, Biden who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:23.010
put their priority on things domestic.
SIERRA: Hmm. Interesting.
&nbsp;

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:31.260
HAASS: Growing opposition in the United States to&nbsp;
any military engagement. The ignominious departure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:36.360
from Afghanistan. So part of it is, I think Mr.&nbsp;
Putin got up in the morning and looked at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:38.760
United States and saw opportunity.
SIERRA: Was ready.
&nbsp;

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:43.140
HAASS: Secondly, he looked at Europe and he saw&nbsp;
the Germans and others, Germans, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:48.000
canceling nuclear power plants, becoming&nbsp;
incredibly dependent on Russian gas. A new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.500
German government after more than a decade of&nbsp;
Angela Merkel and said, "Ah, a bunch of rookies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:56.460
I got a real opportunity in Europe. The French&nbsp;
are worried about elections. The Brits left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:01.920
because of Brexit, left Europe. Europe's in&nbsp;
disarray." Another opportunity. Ukraine, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:05.220
Putin has contempt for Ukraine. And this is where&nbsp;
it gets more interesting. It's not just that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:10.080
has contempt for Ukraine, but you read his essay&nbsp;
from last summer, he writes about Ukraine in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:15.840
almost a mystical way. Talks about fraternalism&nbsp;
and organic relationships between our brotherly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:24.120
Slavs in Ukraine. So clearly sees this as part&nbsp;
of his legacy. Clearly wants to create a sphere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:29.940
of influence there. And because he held Ukraine in&nbsp;
contempt or still does, didn't think it would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:35.400
that hard to achieve. I think also then there’s&nbsp;
other factors. Yes, he was worried or unhappy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:42.000
with, to say the least, the enlargement of NATO.&nbsp;
And even though Ukraine was not on the agenda now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:48.300
this was still a process that from Mr. Putin's&nbsp;
point of view was awful. He never reconciled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:52.560
himself to the breakup of the Soviet Union,&nbsp;
which he termed one of the great historical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:58.320
you know, awful historical moments, tragedies of&nbsp;
the preceding era. He also, I believe is worried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:03.060
about the example that Ukraine sets. That even&nbsp;
though Ukraine is an imperfect democracy, it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:08.580
vibrant democracy. And if you remember in 2014,&nbsp;
Mr. Putin intervened, why? Because the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:14.100
came into the streets of Ukraine in a so-called&nbsp;
colored revolution and chased the autocrat out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:19.920
of the palace, in downtown, what we then called&nbsp;
Kyiv and now we call Kiev. If you're a tyrant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:25.080
like Mr. Putin, there's probably nothing worse&nbsp;
than the precedent of a country that's willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:30.060
to challenge authority. So for all these reasons&nbsp;
and more, and I've even at the risk of really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:35.400
getting into speculation, Gabby, and there's a lot&nbsp;
of speculation out there that Putin's not well and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:40.140
feels to some extent the pressure of time, it&nbsp;
could be any of these things. It could be all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:44.760
of these things. It could be things I haven't&nbsp;
mentioned. My own sense is though he clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:49.860
saw a sense of opportunity. And to me, the most&nbsp;
interesting thing about his assessment as best we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:55.800
can discern it, is how much he underestimated the&nbsp;
challenge. I actually think Vladimir Putin thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:00.000
this would be, to use a phrase that was used in&nbsp;
another war, one undertaken by the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.980
a cakewalk. He looked at Ukraine and said, "Ah,&nbsp;
they'll never put up any serious resistance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:09.540
United States, they don't have the backbone to&nbsp;
stand up to us. Europeans, ah, we've got them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:13.920
literally over a barrel of oil." I think Putin&nbsp;
got up and said, "My own military, look how well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:20.160
we've done in Georgia, Chechnya, Syria, how we did&nbsp;
in Crimea in 2014." I think Putin looked at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:25.380
and said, "This is going to be easy. This is fruit&nbsp;
for the taking." And he decided to go for it.
&nbsp;

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:30.120
SIERRA: It does sound like it should have&nbsp;
been on our radar a little bit then if all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:35.580
of this was sort of you know in the works in his&nbsp;
brain, or you just think that like it was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:38.820
too far fetched for us to imagine it?
HAASS: It's a really good question. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:42.060
you think about intelligence, the business of&nbsp;
intelligence, I don't mean human intelligence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:49.080
but intelligence in governments, so much of it is&nbsp;
influenced by mindset and by assumptions. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:53.220
one of the more infamous moments is obviously&nbsp;
the weapons of mass destruction fiasco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:58.140
when they run up to the Iraq War. I always get a&nbsp;
lot of controversy whenever I talk about it, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:04.440
was in the government then and people legitimately&nbsp;
thought that Saddam Hussein had weapons of mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:08.340
destruction. I thought that even though I was&nbsp;
still opposed to the war, but I thought that. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:13.320
if you think something, it's the old expression,&nbsp;
if you got a hammer, everything looks like a nail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:18.960
Or if you're a doctor and you're looking for this&nbsp;
disease, shockingly enough, you tend to highlight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:25.800
and emphasize those symptoms that correspond to&nbsp;
the disease and you often tend to dismiss or give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:30.720
less weight to symptoms that are not consistent&nbsp;
with a certain diagnosis. So I think a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.520
us just weren't thinking that Putin would ever&nbsp;
do anything so blatant or brazen. In that sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:41.520
it's very much like Saddam in the summer of 1990.&nbsp;
I remember then every Arab government saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:46.020
"Oh, don't overreact you Americans. This is just&nbsp;
theater. This is just diplomacy. He's bluffing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:51.000
he's pressuring Kuwait. He's never going to&nbsp;
invade." He invaded. And I think right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.820
a lot of people just thought that Putin was doing&nbsp;
kind of a modern version of gunboat diplomacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:02.760
was trying to intimidate, was trying to muscle.&nbsp;
Maybe he would do a little bit in the east,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:08.640
but almost all the experts dismissed or at least&nbsp;
discounted what has now become reality. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:12.420
assumptions are powerful things. When the&nbsp;
Bush administration in 2003, went into Iraq,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:16.380
the whole idea that they were going to be met&nbsp;
with children throwing candy instead they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:20.160
met with children throwing Molotov cocktails.&nbsp;
I think Mr. Putin had a little bit of that now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:25.200
There was a sense that our fraternal brothers&nbsp;
in Ukraine were going to welcome us. Well, guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:31.440
what? They're trying to kill Russian soldiers. So&nbsp;
assumptions are powerful and often distorting or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:36.000
dangerous things. And one of the best things I&nbsp;
actually think, whether governments need to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:40.080
and governments do it by what's called red&nbsp;
teaming, they literally have people who look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:45.060
at a problem and tackle every assumption. It's&nbsp;
a good thing though, for people to do in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:49.080
own life when you think about a situation to&nbsp;
say, "Well, what if this were wrong? What if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:52.860
this really important assumption turned out to&nbsp;
be wrong? How would that affect everything that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:57.900
flows from it?" How might I need to change what&nbsp;
I am prepared to do and so forth. And that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:02.640
lesson I take from this, is that mindset and&nbsp;
assumptions can really be dangerous because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:07.440
they can bias you or worse yet, blind you.
SIERRA: I've heard some bad things about assuming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:10.800
so that kind of keeps in the same vein.
HAASS: We're not going to go there.
&nbsp;

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:17.340
SIERRA: So, this is Russia after all and sort&nbsp;
of going back to questions that I think a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:21.360
of people are throwing out there. Is&nbsp;
this the beginning of World War III?
&nbsp;

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:25.500
HAASS: Well, the Biden administration doesn't&nbsp;
want it to be. I mean, nobody wants it to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:31.020
let me put it that way. Because we avoided a world&nbsp;
war during the cold war and certain rules were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:34.260
or unwritten rules were understood&nbsp;
about avoiding direct conflict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:39.060
that we could back proxies and allies, but not&nbsp;
come into direct conflict and nuclear weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:43.200
were there above providing a kind of blanket&nbsp;
over the whole thing. And when Khrushchev,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:48.660
Nikita Khrushchev the Soviet leader backed down in&nbsp;
1962, at the time of the Cuban Missile Crisis, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:54.660
some ways that really reinforced that bias against&nbsp;
the use of nuclear weapons, because any victory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.660 --> 00:14:00.960
would be pyrrhic. Somebody once said the living&nbsp;
would envy the dead. But there were unwritten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:06.000
rules about avoiding conflict directly, because&nbsp;
that could always escalate. Things could get out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:11.220
of hand. And the Biden administration essentially&nbsp;
took that possibility off the table by saying, "We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:16.920
are not going to directly intervene in Ukraine."&nbsp;
And instead, basically developed what you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:22.620
call an indirect strategy that they would arm&nbsp;
Ukraine in many ways, they would introduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:28.920
economic sanctions against Russia and then take&nbsp;
other steps, reinforce NATO, send extra troops to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:34.980
NATO, send oil and gas to Europe so it'd be less&nbsp;
dependent upon Russia. But the whole idea was to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:40.680
and this early on was to increase the deterrence,&nbsp;
to basically shape Mr. Putin's calculations. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:44.820
Mr. Putin would see all these steps or see all&nbsp;
these potential steps like sanctions and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:50.460
"Okay, we're not going to go into Ukraine because&nbsp;
we don't want to trigger all these consequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:55.560
for ourselves." So that was the Biden approach.&nbsp;
But again, the reason to take U.S. forces off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.560 --> 00:15:00.960
table directly in Ukraine, Ukraine is not a NATO&nbsp;
member. United States does not have that kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:07.380
a commitment to Ukraine. And the concern was&nbsp;
that if we were to do it, it could lead as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:12.360
put it to World War III. And also the European&nbsp;
countries were not supportive of it. They too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:16.920
as NATO countries did not want things to go&nbsp;
down that path. So what flowed from it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:21.180
what you might call an indirect strategy, which&nbsp;
was among other things the most important element&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:27.420
of which is to bolster the ability of Ukraine to&nbsp;
resist Russian invasion. And if Russian invasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:32.280
as it now has, has happened all the same, and&nbsp;
even if Russian invasion succeeds, and Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:37.440
takes quote unquote control of the country and&nbsp;
occupies it, that Ukraine then has the ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:42.960
to mount an extremely expensive resistance. And&nbsp;
that's sometimes called the porcupine option or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:47.880
the Afghan option, which is what among others, the&nbsp;
Soviets experienced in Afghanistan and indeed led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:52.920
or helped lead to the end of the Soviet Union.
SIERRA: So, I mean, you mentioned, you know nukes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:55.380
and I think that's another question&nbsp;
sort of floating out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:58.260
is that something we should be worried&nbsp;
about? Is there a chance we can, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:03.720
this escalates that we could get nuked?
HAASS: We didn't think so. And then several days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:09.840
ago, Vladimir Putin put his nuclear weapons on a&nbsp;
higher state of alert. Now again, why he did that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:14.280
we don't know. It might have simply been a&nbsp;
way of intimidating. It might have been his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:20.100
way of saying don't you cross certain lines,&nbsp;
about helping Ukraine. That there's nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:25.080
I wouldn't do. What I found frightening about it&nbsp;
and worrisome about it is not that he's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:30.780
use nuclear weapons. I don't think he will, but&nbsp;
he could. What Mr. Putin has done over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:37.920
two decades is systematically remove the checks&nbsp;
and balances within the Russian government. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:44.100
word I tend to use is deinstitutionalize. And&nbsp;
what he has done is so weakened the bureaucracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:49.680
so weakened any sense of collective leadership&nbsp;
that he's put into his own hands, enormous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:55.740
power. This was his war. This was not Russia's&nbsp;
war. This was Putin's war of choice. He didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.740 --> 00:17:00.180
have to go in. He certainly didn't have to go&nbsp;
in when he did. He certainly doesn't have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:06.180
be following up the way he is. But he enjoys a&nbsp;
degree of discretion that is truly frightening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.180 --> 00:17:11.280
because has one of the world's, in many ways the&nbsp;
world's biggest nuclear arsenal. So when he played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:15.720
that card or waved that card, didn't play that&nbsp;
card, but waved it, I found it alarming. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:21.480
not that I think he's going to use it, but it was&nbsp;
a real signal of how free he is if not to do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:27.000
to for example, pursue a scorched earth policy in&nbsp;
Ukraine with all sorts of conventional weapons of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:32.520
mass destruction. To attack much of the world&nbsp;
using cyber. To maybe widen the war against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:40.080
NATO. To use chemical munitions. That it's not&nbsp;
clear to me who or what inside Russia says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:45.300
"Vladimir, you can't do that. That's a step too&nbsp;
far." And that's at the moment, I don't see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.300 --> 00:17:51.360
institution or set of individuals there. I hope&nbsp;
that it merges. And indeed that might be-
&nbsp;

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:55.200
SIERRA: Me too. That sounds real scary.
HAASS: ... Well, it should. And again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:59.940
if you ask me, are nuclear weapons likely to be&nbsp;
used? No. I think the odds are extraordinarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:02.820
small. I can't dismiss them. 
SIERRA: Right. And there's one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:04.740
person making that decision.
HAASS: ... That's what worries me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:09.300
is the concentration of authority in one set&nbsp;
of hands. And it suggests to me that even if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:13.320
he doesn't go there, he could go to a lot of&nbsp;
other horrible places and dangerous places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:16.860
and destructive places. And by the way, guess&nbsp;
what? He's already doing that. He's doing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:22.860
with this siege of Ukraine cities, and he's&nbsp;
doing it with increasingly indiscriminate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:27.660
use of extraordinarily violent munitions&nbsp;
against civilian targets. These are terror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:32.040
weapons. It's one thing to use precision&nbsp;
guided weapons against a military target.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:37.740
That's what modern war allows you to do. It's&nbsp;
not pretty, but it's what modern war... What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:43.500
Vladimir Putin is doing is purposely trying to&nbsp;
terrorize a civilian population to essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:48.960
psychologically defeat it. He's having trouble&nbsp;
defeating it militarily. What he wants to do is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:53.340
defeat it psychological and politically.
SIERRA: So it sounds like one guy decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.340 --> 00:19:00.120
to go to war and one guy now has&nbsp;
control over all of the next steps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:07.620
and no one to stop him, within Russia.
HAASS: Well, so the question is how could this end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:12.360
in a way, other than the way we don't want it to&nbsp;
end. We don't want Mr. Putin to succeed. We don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:17.580
want him to crush this democracy, this sovereign&nbsp;
country called Ukraine. We don't want to see tens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:22.680
or hundreds of thousands of Ukrainians die. How&nbsp;
else could it end? So two optimistic scenarios.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:28.200
One would be that Mr. Putin, for whatever reason,&nbsp;
decides that the costs outweigh the benefits. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:32.880
risks are too great. And he decides to pursue a&nbsp;
diplomatic option. Talks started the other day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:38.760
made no progress, but that's a possibility. I&nbsp;
think it's at the moment, the longest of long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:43.560
shots. Negotiations only succeed when the&nbsp;
protagonists are willing and able to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:48.000
peace. Mr. Putin is able, I see zero will.&nbsp;
Now, that would have to change. How could it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:51.660
change? Increasing costs on the battlefield,&nbsp;
or and that gets me to the other approach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:57.540
something happens back home, that this combination&nbsp;
of sanctions, maybe the fear of war crimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.540 --> 00:20:03.540
some of the people around him, a really restive&nbsp;
population that can't afford to buy anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:08.340
because the ruble is so weak and they have to&nbsp;
import a lot of their goods. ATMs run out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:12.660
money. They look at their future and go, "Oh my&nbsp;
God, we're going to be pariahs for the rest of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:18.840
life. And I'm only 20 years old. I don't want this&nbsp;
to be my life." That suddenly there is an enormous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:25.860
amount of popular frustration. And at some point,&nbsp;
whether it gets to the point where the police lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:31.740
control, either because the numbers get so great,&nbsp;
or the police lose the will to mow people down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:38.340
The other historical analogy that comes to me here&nbsp;
is 78, 79, in Iran. Revolutions against systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:44.280
against powerful for leaders work when there's&nbsp;
massive public push and the security forces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:49.440
essentially, aren't prepared any longer to kill&nbsp;
their own people in order to defend this regime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:55.320
That's why the Shah became history and was&nbsp;
overthrown just over 40 years ago. We're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:59.520
at that point. Could we get to that point? Yes.&nbsp;
The other thing I guess we could get is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:03.720
part of this inner circle around Putin, people&nbsp;
sitting at the end of that 40 foot table-
&nbsp;

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:05.640
SIERRA: That large table.
HAASS: ... some of them may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:11.640
decide he's out of control. And that he might&nbsp;
actually use nuclear weapons and the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:16.200
States or someone else might do things against&nbsp;
Russia that will set this country back decades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:21.600
That what Putin's going to do will so discredit&nbsp;
all of us, the system will come down. And one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:27.420
has to hope. One has to hope the people with a&nbsp;
degree of proximity and access to Vladimir Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.420 --> 00:21:31.980
are beginning to think this way. One has to hope&nbsp;
they're beginning to talk amongst themselves. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:36.960
risky. Always risky. You're going to be found&nbsp;
out. But one hopes in this combination, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:42.960
of Ukrainian resistance, our ability to retaliate&nbsp;
against certain types of Russian escalation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:48.300
even if we're not going to join the battle in&nbsp;
Ukraine, the economic sanctions, the threat of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:54.720
war crimes, popular dissatisfaction. One has to&nbsp;
hope that this builds some real pressure on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.720 --> 00:22:01.020
home front that either Mr. Putin reconsiders or&nbsp;
people essentially move against Putin. And we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:05.940
not there yet, but I'm hoping we get there.
SIERRA: I have two questions from that. So one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:11.068
is where do the people stand at this point? Is&nbsp;
there a lot of opposition to what's going on-
&nbsp;

00:22:11.068 --> 00:22:13.860
HAASS: In Russia?
SIERRA: ... In Russia? And two is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:17.580
tell me more about sanctions, because I think&nbsp;
a lot of people are talking about them and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:23.460
kind of don't know, you know, do they work?
HAASS: Right. It's hard to get really detailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:28.500
data in the midst of a war, but yes, there's&nbsp;
clearly protests within Russia. Social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:35.340
has not been totally shut down, as say it, often&nbsp;
is in China. I can't go sit here and tell you with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:40.860
confidence that it represents this percent of the&nbsp;
Russian people. I don't think Mr. Putin's done a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:46.140
terribly effective job of selling the necessity&nbsp;
of this war. I don't know how people will react&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:52.320
to images of Russian soldiers coming back as&nbsp;
casualties or dead. What we're already, actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:57.120
beginning to note, Russians are clearly unhappy&nbsp;
with the economic consequences. Thousands, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.120 --> 00:23:01.260
believe have been arrested. Stunningly courageous,&nbsp;
by the way, for these people to stand up. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:06.240
really is civil society in Russia, which is really&nbsp;
admirable. But whether it will ever get to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.240 --> 00:23:11.100
point of being a critical mass, I don't know. But&nbsp;
that's still early days. But that's where we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:16.980
in Russia. Indeed, it's hard not to be struck&nbsp;
by the courage and bravery, both of the society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:21.300
in Ukraine and elements of the society in Russia.&nbsp;
Really, really impressive. And then you asked me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:22.740
another question. Oh, sanctions.
SIERRA: Sanctions.
&nbsp;

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:27.720
HAASS: It's always hard to answer the question, do&nbsp;
sanctions work? So much depends upon how ambitious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.720 --> 00:23:33.060
you define the word work. If you're asking me,&nbsp;
is there a lot about the history of sanctions?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:36.540
And by the way, there's a lot of history of&nbsp;
sanctions, because they're used almost all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:40.740
time. They tend to be the popular tool between&nbsp;
doing nothing and doing everything. So people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:46.800
like it. It's option three. It's what's behind&nbsp;
curtain three. Very rarely do sanctions accomplish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:52.260
big things. Certainly not in a limited amount&nbsp;
of time. The most successful case of sanctions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:57.000
ever is South Africa. Took a long time and then it&nbsp;
only worked because you had large constituencies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.000 --> 00:24:01.500
within South Africa, particularly the business&nbsp;
community and a majority of the population that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:06.180
wanted them to work. I'm not sure any of that&nbsp;
applies here. Sanctions, what they can do is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:10.440
be extraordinarily punitive. They can exact a&nbsp;
toll. And that's what we're seeing in Russia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:15.840
These sanctions are unprecedented. The degree of&nbsp;
a multilateral buy-in we've gotten from Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:21.060
from the European Union, from governments&nbsp;
that historically have been reluctant, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:26.460
Swiss government, German government, and others.&nbsp;
It's quite remarkable this degree of solidarity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.060 --> 00:24:33.420
And again, Putin underestimated the breadth and&nbsp;
depth of the sanctions that were going to hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:38.760
them. These sanctions against the central bank,&nbsp;
against the oligarchs. Now again, it's not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.760 --> 00:24:43.320
to be enough though, I believe directly. It&nbsp;
won't be enough directly to get Putin to stop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:48.360
The more interesting question of sanctions&nbsp;
is whether they create fissures or pressures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:53.580
within Russian society. Putin's probably the&nbsp;
richest man in the world. He's insulated from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.580 --> 00:24:58.560
sanctions. The question is whether he is&nbsp;
insulated sufficiently from the results of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:03.600
sanctions on other people and their discontent as&nbsp;
a result. And that's where time becomes a factor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:07.680
How quickly do sanctions kick in? Not as&nbsp;
quickly in some cases as one would want.
&nbsp;

00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:08.790
SIERRA: But it sounds like&nbsp;
it's pretty immediate.
&nbsp;

00:25:08.790 --> 00:25:13.200
HAASS: Well, some things are. Stock exchange gets&nbsp;
closed regularly now. The Ruble has had dramatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:18.360
fall. There'll be shortage of commodities and so&nbsp;
forth. Now we haven't extended sanctions to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.360 --> 00:25:23.040
energy sector in Russia, because we're worried&nbsp;
about the fallout there. To me though, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:28.920
shorter timeline is what happens on the ground in&nbsp;
Ukraine. To me, the greater need is for more arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:34.080
particularly relevant arms; anti-tank weapons,&nbsp;
anti-aircraft systems to get into Ukraine's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:38.700
hands quickly. We're talking hours and days. We're&nbsp;
not talking about hours and days with sanctions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:43.680
Sanctions tend to play out over a longer time or&nbsp;
horizon. What's critical is what happens literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:48.780
in the next week, on the ground in Ukraine.
SIERRA: Right, okay. Interesting. I feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.780 --> 00:25:53.280
I've got my head around sanctions now, which&nbsp;
is probably a good thing to do. I think also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:56.460
something that people are talking about and you&nbsp;
know sanctions are part of that conversation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:56.460 --> 00:26:00.300
is what about troops? I know you mentioned&nbsp;
there's a pretty specific reason why we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:04.020
not sending in troops, but is that&nbsp;
on the table at all? Will it be?
&nbsp;

00:26:04.020 --> 00:26:08.340
HAASS: President Biden took it off the table&nbsp;
very early on. I can't tell you why. My own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:13.140
sense of it, my own reading of it is that&nbsp;
he didn't think the American people would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.140 --> 00:26:17.640
be behind it. What you're seeing is a real&nbsp;
allergy to foreign military interventions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.640 --> 00:26:22.560
in this country. And also there wasn't European&nbsp;
support for it, to turning it into a NATO war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:29.400
And also because out of concern that it would very&nbsp;
quickly potentially become an east-west, or what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:34.080
used to call an east-west conflict, U.S.-Russian&nbsp;
conflict. And it would've gone against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:37.620
behavior during the Cold War that kept the cold&nbsp;
war cold. We avoided direct confrontations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:43.380
So he opted for what I would call an indirect&nbsp;
strategy. The principal military element, was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.380 --> 00:26:51.900
provide arms and some intelligence to Ukraine and&nbsp;
thought that could sufficiently raise the cost of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:57.000
either invasion or more likely an occupation, was&nbsp;
hoping that would deter Russia. Clearly it didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.000 --> 00:27:04.140
work. Now he's on plan B, which is to provide&nbsp;
enough arms to raise the costs of the invasion and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:10.380
occupation to a level that the Russians don't want&nbsp;
to pay, in lives. It's clearly slowing them down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:10.380 --> 00:27:15.840
The first couple of days of the war did not go&nbsp;
well for Mr. Putin. And the fact that the Russians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:21.780
are escalating with these terrible conventional&nbsp;
mass casualty munitions and by introducing far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:27.300
more troops tells you that, that essentially&nbsp;
what Mr Putin is doing is doubling down. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.300 --> 00:27:31.860
what we need to do, I would argue, is double&nbsp;
down in response and get as many of the relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:37.740
weapons as quickly as we can into Ukraine's&nbsp;
hands. You see these massive convos forming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:43.380
and yes, you can shoot some of them with anti-tank&nbsp;
weapons. There's things you can use, artillery or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.380 --> 00:27:47.820
what have you against trucks. It would be good if&nbsp;
you had some aircraft that could fly to take out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:53.880
some of these convoys. But that's where we are.
SIERRA: So shifting a little to look domestically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:56.880
we do see some Americans and&nbsp;
even some American politicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:56.880 --> 00:28:03.120
supporting Putin. So do you think that&nbsp;
our political polarization sort of hurts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:08.220
our ability to act in situations like these?
HAASS: Again, we're into the land of assessment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:12.720
here. My own sense is it contributed to Mr.&nbsp;
Putin's confidence. That the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.720 --> 00:28:14.329
would be too divided to mount a-
SIERRA: All that disarray.
&nbsp;

00:28:14.329 --> 00:28:20.040
HAASS: ... an effective response. And he had&nbsp;
gotten used to, in particular, working with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.040 --> 00:28:24.540
president Trump and people around him who had&nbsp;
a, let's just call it what it is, a pro-Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:29.460
view of the world. Some of these people said the&nbsp;
real enemy isn't Russia, the real enemy is China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:35.220
So my own sense is he thought we would be too&nbsp;
divided to mount an effective political response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.220 --> 00:28:39.360
which is a prerequisite for the rest of our&nbsp;
response, from military, from sending arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:46.320
to sanctions, what have you, and he was wrong.&nbsp;
That what we see is most Americans are rallying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:49.980
around this president, who by the way is not a&nbsp;
popular president. The most recent polls still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:49.980 --> 00:28:51.893
show Mr. Biden, what, south of 40%?
SIERRA: Yeah. Yeah.
&nbsp;

00:28:51.893 --> 00:28:56.820
HAASS: In favorable ratings, going into the&nbsp;
state of the union. But most people seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.820 --> 00:29:03.780
supportive. This is pretty black or white. This is&nbsp;
pretty democracy versus evil. So much of life is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:10.080
the fifth 51/49 kind of decision. This is not at&nbsp;
the 51/49 yard lines. This is, we got end zones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:10.080 --> 00:29:15.420
This is pretty clear cut. What you're seeing is&nbsp;
a good number of Republicans coming around to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.420 --> 00:29:20.520
supporting the administration. They're not taking&nbsp;
the political step of criticizing Donald Trump.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:26.220
For a lot of them that would be a bridge too far.&nbsp;
But they're clearly distancing themselves from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.220 --> 00:29:33.060
Trump-like policy, which has been an enabling of&nbsp;
Russia and sympathetic to Russia for the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:39.060
what, five years. So some Republicans can't bring&nbsp;
themselves to support a democratic president even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:43.620
when he is right. And I'll be honest, I've worked&nbsp;
for three Republican presidents, one democratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.620 --> 00:29:46.860
president. I think the Biden administration has&nbsp;
handled this well for the most part. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:51.300
they do deserve support, do deserve praise.&nbsp;
For some Republicans that's a bridge too far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.300 --> 00:29:54.540
but some Republicans are. I think actually are&nbsp;
supporting them. I actually think there is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.540 --> 00:29:57.529
degree of bipartisanship that has-
SIERRA: Come around.
&nbsp;

00:29:57.529 --> 00:30:01.500
HAASS: ... come around here. So there's still a&nbsp;
recognizable Republican party somewhere there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:07.620
A bit in camouflage, a bit buried. But it may be&nbsp;
down, but it's not out. And you listen to somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.620 --> 00:30:12.300
like Mitt Romney and it's there in full force&nbsp;
and Mitt Romney's the guy who years ago said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.300 --> 00:30:18.120
"Russia is the biggest threat." And looks rather,&nbsp;
shall we say vindicated? On the democratic side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:22.800
there's a lot more support, not complete,&nbsp;
but the bulk of the democratic party in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.800 --> 00:30:27.240
surprising amount of the, particularly the&nbsp;
Washington or institutional Republican party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:27.240 --> 00:30:34.740
on this issue seems to be supporting our policy&nbsp;
if not supporting President Biden per se. If not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.740 --> 00:30:38.460
opposing former President Trump, per se.
SIERRA: You know you mentioned a couple of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:38.460 --> 00:30:44.100
times people coming together, do you think&nbsp;
that this war could change anything about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.100 --> 00:30:47.340
the world works? Or do you think we're just&nbsp;
going to go back to business as usual?
&nbsp;

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:54.240
HAASS: I think there's a couple of silver linings&nbsp;
already. One is the reemergence of Europe. Both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.240 --> 00:30:58.440
the European Union, as well as the transatlantic&nbsp;
relationship in NATO, plus this new German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.440 --> 00:31:03.540
government. What's happened in recent days and&nbsp;
weeks, if we'd had this conversation a month ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.540 --> 00:31:08.460
I would've said, "That'd be great, but ain't&nbsp;
going to happen." So remarkable turnaround,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:13.140
in a stunningly small amount of time. Europe has&nbsp;
really stepped up. The fact that this new German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:13.140 --> 00:31:17.820
government is talking about spending 2% or more&nbsp;
of its GDP on defense, we couldn't get them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.820 --> 00:31:23.040
do that for decades. There's a real recognition&nbsp;
I think that Mr. Putin represents something very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:27.060
different and very dangerous. And that this&nbsp;
Putin is perhaps different than the Putin we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:31.860
thought we knew. I think economic sanctions have&nbsp;
been shown to be more potentially effective than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.860 --> 00:31:36.240
people thought. And I'm hoping that influences&nbsp;
Chinese behavior on something like Taiwan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:36.240 --> 00:31:41.400
That the Chinese will say to themselves, "Wow.&nbsp;
Look at the price that the Russians have to pay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.400 --> 00:31:45.540
Look how difficult it is for them." By the way,&nbsp;
the Americans aren't intervening in Ukraine. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.540 --> 00:31:50.100
the Americans may well, I believe, would intervene&nbsp;
in a Taiwan contingency. So I think for China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.640 --> 00:31:55.800
this has to get them to think more than&nbsp;
twice about any dreams they may have had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:57.900
of invading Taiwan.
SIERRA: Interesting.
&nbsp;

00:31:57.900 --> 00:32:02.040
HAASS: I don't know, in our country. I'm&nbsp;
hopeful that this will do something to revive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:02.040 --> 00:32:07.020
both bipartisanship a bit, but also a sense&nbsp;
of the importance of democracy and freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.620 --> 00:32:12.900
and values. That what we are seeing is&nbsp;
there's still evil in the world. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:17.400
that countries that believe in democracy and&nbsp;
freedom have to stand up and work together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:24.300
And what I'm also hoping is it discredits people&nbsp;
in this country who have cozied up to Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:28.620
as well as in places like Europe who have cozied&nbsp;
up to Russia, the far right in France. That it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:34.920
discredits them. And I'm hoping that one of the&nbsp;
unanticipated positive consequences of Mr. Putin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:39.120
this is a great irony here, here's this guy who&nbsp;
did all this to, in social media and elsewhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.120 --> 00:32:44.880
to undermine American democracy. He may have&nbsp;
actually revived it a little bit. He may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:49.680
actually reminded us about why democracy&nbsp;
matters and why we need to work together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:52.920
sometimes. And that would be the-
SIERRA: And that it's not assumed.
&nbsp;

00:32:52.920 --> 00:32:57.120
HAASS: Don't assume anything. That is the lesson&nbsp;
of the last few years, whether you're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:01.440
about foreign policy or domestic policy&nbsp;
or American democracy or anything else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:06.120
Don't assume anything.
SIERRA: Anything. Well then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:11.340
with all of this, why does this matter?
HAASS: I think it's the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:11.340 --> 00:33:15.660
question. That we have to see the&nbsp;
connections between what is going on here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:23.400
And this is to me, the basic lesson of history.&nbsp;
Everything we do as a society, as an economy, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.400 --> 00:33:29.580
individuals, depends upon there being order in the&nbsp;
world. Think about how our lives were disrupted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.580 --> 00:33:37.200
a virus. Think about how our lives were disrupted&nbsp;
by a handful of terrorists on 9/11. Obviously, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:37.200 --> 00:33:43.920
our parents or grandparents generations about how&nbsp;
our lives were disrupted by World War II. Order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:50.760
out there is something that we take for granted at&nbsp;
our peril, because everything we are as a society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:50.760 --> 00:33:57.120
as families, as businesses, as economies,&nbsp;
depends on our ability to go to work safely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:57.120 --> 00:34:04.080
go to school safely and so forth. And the&nbsp;
most basic feature of that order in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.080 --> 00:34:08.340
world is the idea that countries, governments,&nbsp;
which have the most capability in their hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:14.100
don't invade other countries. That they respect&nbsp;
borders. And the last time this was ignored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:14.100 --> 00:34:18.420
wholesale was World War II. It gets ignored every&nbsp;
now and then since then, but never wholesale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:18.960 --> 00:34:26.220
This is the most basic tenant, principle, norm,&nbsp;
this thread of the fabric, of order in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:26.220 --> 00:34:31.440
And if Mr. Putin is allowed to get away with&nbsp;
what he's doing, why does anyone think, one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:37.140
that he will stop there? In my experience, people&nbsp;
on a roll rarely stop. It's kind of Newton's Law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:37.140 --> 00:34:41.940
objects in motion remain in motion. And then&nbsp;
secondly, why do we think others wouldn't follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:49.380
suit? Why do we think others wouldn't take notes?&nbsp;
Interestingly enough, when we in 1990, stood up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:49.380 --> 00:34:53.820
to Saddam Hussein in Operation Desert Storm,&nbsp;
that was the logic. What was different then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:53.820 --> 00:34:58.800
it was at the dawn of the post Cold War era.&nbsp;
The wall had just come down a year before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:06.120
the Berlin wall. And President Bush, the father&nbsp;
essentially said, How we react to this, what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:06.120 --> 00:35:11.340
do and don't do will, will shape the character of&nbsp;
the next era of history." And we thought that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:11.340 --> 00:35:15.720
Saddam got away with it, not only would Saddam&nbsp;
come to dominate the Middle East and its energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.720 --> 00:35:22.080
and all that, but we said others would take notes&nbsp;
and there would be other Saddams out there. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:26.820
would calculate they could get away with it in&nbsp;
every part of the world. That's where we are now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:33.600
It's not just what Vladimir Putin would do if he&nbsp;
gets on a roll, what he might do about NATO and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:38.700
other countries in Europe. But second of all, if&nbsp;
we did nothing, what might China then do about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:38.700 --> 00:35:44.100
Taiwan. Or what an Iran might do or North Korea.&nbsp;
And countries that have been dependent on us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.100 --> 00:35:48.420
who look to us for help would say, "Wow, we&nbsp;
can't depend on these guys. They did nothing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:49.260 --> 00:35:54.480
So either we're going to have to sell out, defer&nbsp;
to our powerful neighbor and kind of let them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:54.480 --> 00:35:58.500
run our lives, which is what Russia wanted&nbsp;
with Ukraine. Or we're going to have to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:58.500 --> 00:36:02.520
get nuclear weapons of our own." Think about&nbsp;
that, if suddenly we have a world where everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.520 --> 00:36:06.540
decides they need to scramble to get their own&nbsp;
nuclear weapons and what happens the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.540 --> 00:36:11.340
there's a crisis. So that more than anything&nbsp;
else is why this matters. I know words like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:11.340 --> 00:36:14.160
world order sound terribly wonky-
SIERRA: Yeah. And I was going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:17.340
say also a little abstract to give to&nbsp;
just someone when they're like, "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:21.780
why does this hit me at home?" Like you mentioned&nbsp;
9/11 and COVID, those things directly affected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:21.780 --> 00:36:26.340
people right here. Whereas you're saying,&nbsp;
"Oh, people will take notes, the world order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.940 --> 00:36:29.813
there's all this." I think that's a little&nbsp;
more... It's just not as direct for people.
&nbsp;

00:36:29.813 --> 00:36:33.240
HAASS: When I say take notes, take notes is not&nbsp;
all they're going to do. They're then going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:37.260
take those notes and they're going to invade&nbsp;
neighbors. They're going to develop nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:37.260 --> 00:36:42.360
weapons. They're going to stamp out democracy and&nbsp;
freedom. And why do we think we would be immune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:49.320
potentially from the consequences of their actions&nbsp;
or aggression? One of the lessons in this world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:49.320 --> 00:36:54.180
this show is about, why does it matter, well, the&nbsp;
basic lesson of living in an interconnected world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:54.180 --> 00:37:00.360
is that almost everything matters. There ain't&nbsp;
no there versus here. They are interwoven. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:05.220
we're naive if we think that awful stuff can&nbsp;
happen and then it gets compartmentalized. 
&nbsp;

00:37:05.220 --> 00:37:08.340
SIERRA: Sure.
HAASS: It'll spread. So there's that. We also care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.340 --> 00:37:11.580
about things like freedom and human life.
SIERRA: Of course.
&nbsp;

00:37:11.580 --> 00:37:16.920
HAASS: Foreign policy isn't just cold blooded,&nbsp;
realpolitik. It isn't just about quote unquote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.920 --> 00:37:22.140
“order.” It's also about human lives. It's&nbsp;
about freedom, and it's about democracy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:27.180
values. And we don't want to see good people&nbsp;
left in the lurch. One way or another we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:27.180 --> 00:37:32.160
to help them. In this case indirectly, by giving&nbsp;
them economic and military help and diplomatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:36.480
help and everything else. In some cases we might&nbsp;
want to help them directly. And that's a tactical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.480 --> 00:37:40.560
decision we have to make. But we believe in&nbsp;
things that are bigger than ourselves. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.560 --> 00:37:44.820
was a country founded on an idea. That's the&nbsp;
whole concept that's at the core of American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.820 --> 00:37:50.820
nationhood. We believe in ideas. We believe&nbsp;
in values. And so I believe American foreign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:50.820 --> 00:37:55.620
policy has to worry about things like order&nbsp;
because that will affect us. That's real stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:56.280 --> 00:38:01.740
That will affect our interests, our security, our&nbsp;
prosperity, all that matters. That's real stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:01.740 --> 00:38:07.440
But also foreign policy should be about it more&nbsp;
than that. We are against genocides, even if we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:07.440 --> 00:38:12.600
not dying in the genocide. Why? Because we believe&nbsp;
genocide is wrong. We believe in human rights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:17.280
even if it's not Americans whose human rights will&nbsp;
be affected for the better, because we believe in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:21.600
those things. So foreign policy's always got&nbsp;
to be a matter of interest, but also values.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:21.600 --> 00:38:27.360
And I would argue it gets in trouble when it's&nbsp;
one or the other. It can't just be a crusade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:27.360 --> 00:38:31.920
on behalf of values, but it just can't be cold&nbsp;
blooded, narrow interest. It's got to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:31.920 --> 00:38:38.280
balance of the two. So that's why this matters.
SIERRA: Well, I think this was extremely helpful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.280 --> 00:38:43.260
for me and I'm sure it will be for a lot of&nbsp;
other people. So thank you for joining us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.260 --> 00:38:49.620
Hopefully next time maybe we could talk about&nbsp;
something really happy, versus always seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:52.440
you under these circumstances.
HAASS: I hope it's about golf.
&nbsp;

00:38:52.440 --> 00:38:54.420
SIERRA: One can dream. Hope.
HAASS: ... One can dream.
&nbsp;

00:38:54.420 --> 00:38:56.980
SIERRA: Thank you.
